<https://github.com/john1hsu/critical-thinking-graph/id/reversal-test#graph> {
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/critical-thinking-graph/id/reversal-test> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/created> "2026-11-12"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/critical-thinking-graph/id/reversal-test> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/critical-thinking-graph/ontology#Technique> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/critical-thinking-graph/id/reversal-test> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Reversal test" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/critical-thinking-graph/id/reversal-test> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "Bostrom-Ord reversal test" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/critical-thinking-graph/id/reversal-test> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "double reversal test" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/critical-thinking-graph/id/reversal-test> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader> <https://github.com/john1hsu/critical-thinking-graph/id/debiasing> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/critical-thinking-graph/id/reversal-test> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A check for status quo bias: when a change to some quantity seems bad, ask whether the opposite change would also be bad. If both directions seem worse, you owe a reason why the current value happens to be the best one; lacking it, suspect the preference comes from bias." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/critical-thinking-graph/id/reversal-test> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> <https://github.com/john1hsu/critical-thinking-graph/id/scheme/debiasing-and-metacognition> .
