<https://github.com/john1hsu/critical-thinking-graph/id/ecological-rationality#graph> {
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/critical-thinking-graph/id/ecological-rationality> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/created> "2026-11-10"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/critical-thinking-graph/id/ecological-rationality> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/critical-thinking-graph/ontology#Concept> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/critical-thinking-graph/id/ecological-rationality> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Ecological rationality" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/critical-thinking-graph/id/ecological-rationality> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "environment-relative rationality" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/critical-thinking-graph/id/ecological-rationality> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "mind-environment fit" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/critical-thinking-graph/id/ecological-rationality> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader> <https://github.com/john1hsu/critical-thinking-graph/id/bounded-rationality> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/critical-thinking-graph/id/ecological-rationality> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The view, developed by Gerd Gigerenzer, Peter Todd and the ABC Research Group, that a decision strategy is rational to the degree that it fits the information structure of the environment it is used in, so the same simple heuristic can be smart in one setting and error-prone in another." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/critical-thinking-graph/id/ecological-rationality> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> <https://github.com/john1hsu/critical-thinking-graph/id/scheme/heuristics-and-dual-process> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/critical-thinking-graph/id/ecological-rationality> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Ecological rationality" .
